          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many coats should stamped concrete get?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Two thin coats are standard for new or stripped stamped concrete. For routine resealing of a sound film, one thin coat is often enough. More coats build a thick layer that can cloud, peel and become slippery, so resist adding extra layers just to boost gloss."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why did my stamped concrete sealer turn white?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "White haze usually means moisture is trapped in or under the film. Common causes are sealing damp concrete, rain or dew before the sealer cured, heavy coats, or too much buildup. With solvent acrylic, re-dissolving with the appropriate solvent often clears it. Water-based haze may require stripping and resealing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I seal stamped concrete myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Cleaning, drying and applying two thin coats with a pump sprayer and roller is a manageable weekend project for most patios. Call a pro if the existing sealer needs stripping, the surface needs recoloring, or there is widespread clouding or peeling that you are not comfortable correcting with solvents."
          }
