        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Stamped concrete is a painted finish as much as a concrete slab. The color hardener and antiquing release that make it look like slate or flagstone sit in the top layer, and without a sealer they fade, chalk and stain within a couple of seasons. That is why nearly every stamped patio gets an acrylic film, and why the choice of film, how thin you lay it, and how you keep it from turning slick decide whether the patio looks good for years or becomes a resealing chore.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-stamped-is-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Stamped Concrete Needs a Different Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plain concrete can be sealed for protection alone. Stamped concrete is sealed for appearance too. Its color comes from integral pigment, a dry-shake color hardener troweled into the surface, and a contrasting release agent left in the textured lows. Those layers are thin. Sun, foot traffic and weather fade and wear them, and a film sealer is what locks them in and gives them depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the usual advice for driveways, a penetrating repellent, rarely satisfies stamped patio owners. A repellent protects the concrete from water and salt, but leaves the color muted and the release exposed. Our general <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers when penetrating products make sense; for stamped work, an acrylic film is the default.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture also changes the job. Deep grout lines and textured lows hold extra sealer, which can pool and dry thick. Thick spots trap moisture and are the first to turn white. Applying stamped concrete sealer is about keeping coats thin and even across a surface that is anything but flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, stamped surfaces are usually patios, pool decks and walkways, all places people walk barefoot or in wet shoes. Sheen and slip resistance matter more here than on any other concrete you own, which is why we treat traction as its own decision below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solvent-or-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Solvent-Based or Water-Based Acrylic
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solvent-based acrylics are the traditional choice for stamped concrete. They penetrate slightly before forming a film, deliver the richest color enhancement, and re-dissolve when a new coat or a solvent wipe goes on. That last trait is a big deal: it means recoats fuse with the old layer, and cloudy patches can often be cleared without stripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based acrylics are lower odor, easier to clean up and allowed everywhere, including areas with strict VOC limits. They typically give a little less color depth and do not re-dissolve the same way, so fixing clouding or recoating over a worn film is less forgiving. They are a good choice for new work or in regions where solvent options are restricted.
          </p>

