            NaN. Why Stamped Concrete Needs a Different Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plain concrete can be sealed for protection alone. Stamped concrete is sealed for appearance too. Its color comes from integral pigment, a dry-shake color hardener troweled into the surface, and a contrasting release agent left in the textured lows. Those layers are thin. Sun, foot traffic and weather fade and wear them, and a film sealer is what locks them in and gives them depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the usual advice for driveways, a penetrating repellent, rarely satisfies stamped patio owners. A repellent protects the concrete from water and salt, but leaves the color muted and the release exposed. Our general <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers when penetrating products make sense; for stamped work, an acrylic film is the default.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture also changes the job. Deep grout lines and textured lows hold extra sealer, which can pool and dry thick. Thick spots trap moisture and are the first to turn white. Applying stamped concrete sealer is about keeping coats thin and even across a surface that is anything but flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, stamped surfaces are usually patios, pool decks and walkways, all places people walk barefoot or in wet shoes. Sheen and slip resistance matter more here than on any other concrete you own, which is why we treat traction as its own decision below.
          </p>
