          </p>

          <p>
            Shaded patios have the opposite problem. They stay damp longer after rain and dew, grow mildew in the textured lows, and are more prone to white haze because the slab rarely dries out completely. Wash with a mildew cleaner, give the surface extra drying time before sealing, and lean toward thinner coats or a breathable water-based product if clouding has been a repeat problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold climates add freeze-thaw and deicer exposure. A sound acrylic film protects the surface well while it is intact, but chips and worn patches let water in, and the freeze damage starts there. Before winter, touch up any worn spots, use plastic shovels rather than steel blades, and spread deicers sparingly. Our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt guide</a> lists products that are easier on decorative concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot, sunny climates break down acrylic faster through UV exposure. South- and west-facing patios typically fade and dull ahead of shaded areas on the same slab. Resealing just the sun-exposed zones between full resealings keeps the color even without building up excess film where it is not needed. Seal early in the morning, when the slab is coolest, to avoid bubbles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reseal-and-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. Reseal Cycle and What It Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most stamped concrete needs resealing every two to three years, sooner on sunny, high-traffic or pool-side areas. The signs are fading color, dull patches, water that no longer beads, and visible wear lanes. Resealing on time prevents release color from wearing away, which cannot be restored without recoloring.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges at the Time of Writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common reseal cycle for stamped acrylic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">150-300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, first coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin coats beat one heavy coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cure before sealing new work</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Material costs for DIY resealing are modest. A 400-square-foot patio usually needs a few gallons for two thin coats, plus an anti-slip additive and cleaning supplies. Premium solvent acrylics cost more per gallon than budget products but often last longer and are easier to repair, which lowers cost per year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional resealing costs more and varies by region, but includes cleaning, minor repairs and the right equipment. A pro is worth calling if the patio needs stripping, has severe clouding, or needs recoloring with tinted sealer. For simple cleaning and resealing, most owners can do it in a weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your stamped concrete is part of a driveway rather than a patio, expect faster wear from tires and salt. Our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">driveway sealer reviews</a> cover those conditions. Stained patios meant to look like stone have similar needs; see our guide on <a href="/how-to-stain-concrete-patio-to-look-like-stone/">staining a concrete patio to look like stone</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing too soon after washing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stamped texture holds water in grout lines and lows long after the surface looks dry. Sealing over that hidden moisture traps it under the film and causes white haze. Wait at least a full day or two of dry weather and use a plastic sheet moisture test on low spots before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying thick coats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat fills texture, increases slip risk and traps solvent and moisture, which leads to clouding and bubbles. Two thin coats at the label's coverage rate produce a better finish and last longer. Back-roll puddles in grout lines before they dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing solvent and water-based sealers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent acrylic can lift or wrinkle a water-based film, and water-based products may not bond to old solvent sealer. Identify what is already on the patio, or test a hidden patch and check adhesion, before resealing the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping grit on wet areas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glossy acrylic on pool decks, steps and sloped walks can become dangerously slick when wet. Mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat on every surface people walk on wet, and keep stirring during application so the grit stays evenly distributed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing too hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High pressure and a narrow tip can etch stamped texture and strip release color, leaving lighter streaks that show through the new sealer. Use moderate pressure, a wide fan tip or a surface cleaner, and keep the nozzle moving at a consistent distance.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which type of sealer suits stamped concrete best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most stamped patios, a solvent-based acrylic is the best fit. It enhances color, bonds well when recoated, and cloudiness can often be fixed with solvent rather than stripping. Water-based acrylics are a good alternative where VOC rules apply or odor is a concern. Penetrating repellents protect but leave colors muted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats should stamped concrete get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats are standard for new or stripped stamped concrete. For routine resealing of a sound film, one thin coat is often enough. More coats build a thick layer that can cloud, peel and become slippery, so resist adding extra layers just to boost gloss.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my stamped concrete sealer turn white?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                White haze usually means moisture is trapped in or under the film. Common causes are sealing damp concrete, rain or dew before the sealer cured, heavy coats, or too much buildup. With solvent acrylic, re-dissolving with the appropriate solvent often clears it. Water-based haze may require stripping and resealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal stamped concrete myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Cleaning, drying and applying two thin coats with a pump sprayer and roller is a manageable weekend project for most patios. Call a pro if the existing sealer needs stripping, the surface needs recoloring, or there is widespread clouding or peeling that you are not comfortable correcting with solvents.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I make sealed stamped concrete less slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat, keep coats thin to preserve texture, and choose a satin or semi-gloss sheen instead of high gloss. If an older patio is already slick from buildup, partially stripping and applying one fresh coat with grit can restore traction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I change the color when resealing stamped concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some sealers are available tinted, which can refresh or shift faded colors. Tinted sealer adds color in the film, so it wears with the film and needs careful, even application to avoid streaks. Test on a small area first, and keep in mind that tinted sealer hides texture slightly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does stamped concrete need sealing every year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most stamped patios need resealing every two to three years. High-sun, pool-side or high-traffic areas may need it sooner. Annual heavy resealing builds up too much film. Instead, clean the surface each year and reseal when color fades or water stops beading.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

